// Generates the two files the website serves at its root so that a language
// model, or an agent acting for one, can find and read Tessera's reference
// without crawling the repository. The convention is https://llmstxt.org :
//
//   llms.txt       a short index: what Tessera is, and where the documents are
//   llms-full.txt  the documentation itself, in one file
//
// `llms-full.txt` is generated from harness/reference/tessera.md, which is the
// reference the measurement harness gives a model and nothing else, so what an
// agent reads on the website is exactly what the harness measures.
//
//   tessera run tools/make_llms.tes -- web/llms.txt web/llms-full.txt harness/reference/tessera.md
//
// Written for the stage 0 compiler (draft 0.3), like tools/embed_runtime.tes,
// so it runs before stage 1 exists: no interpolation, no modules.

fn site() -> Text = "https://tessera-language.com"

fn repository() -> Text = "https://github.com/dib0/Tessera-language"

fn blob() -> Text = "https://github.com/dib0/Tessera-language/blob/main"

fn tree() -> Text = "https://github.com/dib0/Tessera-language/tree/main"

note "The index file. Per the convention: one title, one blockquote summary, then sections of links, with `Optional` last for a reader that is short of context."
fn index_text() -> Text {
let lines = [
"# Tessera",
"",
"> Tessera is a programming language for software that language models write and people review. It has a small, regular syntax; a function declares what it can do with `uses` and is pure without it; a `note` keeps the reason for a decision next to the code; and programs compile to safe Rust. Its design decisions are made by measurement, not by taste: a harness has models solve tasks from the reference alone, compiles every answer, and records pass rate and token cost per variant of the language.",
"",
"Tessera is an early proof of concept under the MIT licence. The syntax and the compiler are not stable, and Tessera must not be relied on as a security guarantee.",
"",
"To write Tessera, read the language reference below first. It is the whole language in one file, and it is what the harness gives a model.",
"",
"## Docs",
"",
"- [Language reference](" + site() + "/llms-full.txt): the complete language in one file: types, effects, syntax, the standard library, the diagnostics and the runtime errors.",
"- [Why Tessera](" + site() + "/why): the argument for a language aimed at models that people still review.",
"- [The language](" + site() + "/language): a guided tour with examples.",
"- [How it works](" + site() + "/how): the compilers, the runtime and the measurement harness.",
"- [Get Tessera](" + site() + "/get): building the toolchain and compiling a first program.",
"",
"## Source",
"",
"- [Repository](" + repository() + "): specification, both compilers, runtime, standard modules, harness and examples.",
"- [Specification](" + blob() + "/language-design.md): the authoritative description of the language. Where the compiler and the specification disagree, the specification wins.",
"- [Examples](" + tree() + "/examples): complete programs, from a few lines to a game.",
"- [Standard modules](" + tree() + "/std): the modules that ship with the compiler, written in Tessera.",
"",
"## Optional",
"",
"- [Measurement harness](" + tree() + "/harness): the tasks, the variants and the method behind the design decisions.",
"- [Tesseradoom](" + tree() + "/examples/tesseradoom): a DOOM-style engine in Tessera, the largest example and the test bed for new features.",
"- [Web hosts](" + blob() + "/hosts/README.md): running a Tessera web application as CGI, FastCGI, a server, or a module inside nginx, Apache or IIS.",
"",
]
join(lines, "\n")
}

note "The reference without its own title line, so that the generated file has one heading instead of two."
fn without_title(reference: Text) -> Text {
var out: List[Int] = []
var past_title = false
for code in code_points(reference) {
if past_title {
set out = append(out, code)
} else if code == 10 {
set past_title = true
}
}
if past_title {
return from_code_points(out)
}
reference
}

note "The full reference, with a header that says where it came from and that it is generated."
fn full_text(reference: Text) -> Text {
let header = [
"# Tessera language reference",
"",
"> Everything needed to write correct Tessera, in one file. This is the reference",
"> the measurement harness gives a model, and nothing else: no other documentation",
"> and no way to run code. Tessera is an early proof of concept under the MIT",
"> licence; the syntax and the compiler are not stable yet.",
">",
"> Source: " + repository(),
"> Specification: " + blob() + "/language-design.md",
"",
"Generated from harness/reference/tessera.md; do not edit by hand.",
"",
"---",
"",
]
join(header, "\n") + without_title(reference)
}

fn main() -> Unit uses io, fs, env {
let arguments = args()
if length(arguments) < 3 {
print_error("usage: make_llms <llms.txt> <llms-full.txt> <reference.md>")
exit(2)
}
let index_path = arguments[0]
let full_path = arguments[1]
let reference_path = arguments[2]
if not file_exists(reference_path) {
print_error("error: cannot read " + reference_path)
exit(2)
}
write_file(index_path, index_text())
write_file(full_path, full_text(read_file(reference_path)))
print("wrote " + index_path + " and " + full_path)
}
